    "address_form": "Use the owner-approved form of address. Mexican consumer and marketing web copy defaults to informal tu more readily than Spain does; default to tu only when no project rule says otherwise, and record the assumption. Formal/institutional or B2B content should default to usted instead -- this is a project-level decision, not a seed default.",
    "plural_you": "Mexican Spanish uses ustedes for plural 'you' in all registers. Never use vosotros/vosotras or their verb forms; those are Spain-only.",
    "typography": "Keep Spanish inverted question and exclamation marks (¿ ¡) at the start of questions and exclamations. Never alter code, URLs, placeholders, identifiers, or locked text.",
    "numbers": "Mexico conventionally uses a decimal point and a comma for thousands (1,234.56), matching US formatting -- the opposite of Spain's comma-decimal convention. Do not silently reformat a number that is already a protected token (a price, an ID, a measurement) without a project rule.",
    "brands_and_identifiers": "Preserve unless an owner-approved localized form exists.",
    "seo": "Adapt Mexican search-query intent and vocabulary (see vocabulary entries below). Do not translate English keywords mechanically."
